  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "At the bar, Max complains to Kostya about how many times Vika has turned him down, right before Nagiyev gathers the kitchen brigade to introduce the new molecular chef from Paris.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Пора самому научиться открывать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Слушай, да я учусь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Только счета за обучение большие.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "А как у тебя с Викой сейчас?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сорок раз приглашал в кино.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пять раз говорил, что не могу без неё жить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "И один раз пытался поцеловать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я случайно подслушал разговор по телефону.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А, интересно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Я обещал, что найду повара.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Я это сделал. Прошу знакомиться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Екатерина Семёнова окончила академию.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Пора самому научиться открывать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It is time to learn to open it yourself.' Kostya anchors the line on 'it's time', while 'open' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Слушай, да я учусь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Listen, I am learning.' The grammar packs 'listen' and 'study' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Только счета за обучение большие.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Only the bills for the training are big.' Max anchors the line on 'only', while 'big' completes the thought. Kostya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "А как у тебя с Викой сейчас?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And how are things with Vika for you now?' Kostya raises the pitch on 'now' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сорок раз приглашал в кино.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I invited her to the cinema forty times.' The meaning hinges on 'forty' working together with 'cinema' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пять раз говорил, что не могу без неё жить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Five times I said that I cannot live without her.' Notice how Russian expresses 'five' and 'to live' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "И один раз пытался поцеловать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And once I tried to kiss her.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'and' before landing on 'to kiss'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я случайно подслушал разговор по телефону.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I accidentally overheard a conversation on the phone.' In Russian, the idea of 'I' flows naturally into 'phone' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А, интересно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Ah, interesting.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'and' before landing on 'interesting'. Dmitry Nagiyev answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Я обещал, что найду повара.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I promised that I would find a cook.' Dmitry Nagiyev anchors the line on 'I', while 'cook' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Я это сделал. Прошу знакомиться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I did it. Please meet her.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'I' before landing on 'to get acquainted'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Екатерина Семёнова окончила академию.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yekaterina Semyonova graduated from the academy.' Notice how Russian expresses 'Semyonova' and 'academy' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
